{# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   SUPPORT PROMPT — one component, dropped wherever people get stuck.

   ── WHY THIS EXISTS AS A PARTIAL ────────────────────────────────────────────

   Support that only lives at /support is support that only reaches people who
   already believe somebody can help. The person whose payment has just stalled
   is looking at a payment page, not at a help menu, and what they do next is
   decided by what is in front of them — refresh, give up, or tell their friends
   they were robbed. So the offer has to be on THAT page.

   ── AND WHY IT CARRIES THE REFERENCE ────────────────────────────────────────

   `sp_ref` is the whole point. Handing somebody a support link is asking them to
   re-explain a problem the page already knows about, and to copy a forty-
   character reference by hand — which is where the digits get dropped and the
   lookup fails and they conclude, reasonably, that nothing works. With the
   reference in the link, the assistant opens already holding it and can go
   straight to the repair.

   ── PARAMETERS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     sp_ref      payment reference to hand over, if the page has one
     sp_kind     'payment' | 'votes' | 'account' | 'general'  (default general)
     sp_title    override the heading
     sp_body     override the sentence
     sp_compact  true for a single line rather than a card
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
{% set _kind  = sp_kind|default('general') %}
{% set _ref   = sp_ref|default('') %}
{% set _href  = '/support/assistant?topic=' ~ _kind
                ~ (_ref ? '&ref=' ~ _ref|url_encode ~ '&ask=1' : '') %}

{% set _title = sp_title|default({
  'payment': 'Paid, but nothing has arrived?',
  'votes':   'Votes not showing?',
  'account': 'Trouble with your account?',
  'general': 'Something not right?'
}[_kind]) %}

{% set _body = sp_body|default({
  'payment': _ref
    ? 'The assistant can re-check this payment with the gateway right now and credit the votes if the money went through. It takes a few seconds and you do not need an account.'
    : 'The assistant can re-check a payment with the gateway and credit the votes if the money went through — have your reference to hand.',
  'votes':   'If a payment went through and the votes never appeared, the assistant can put that right on the spot.',
  'account': 'The assistant can check what the platform is doing and pass anything it cannot settle to a person.',
  'general': 'The assistant can check the live state of the platform, fix a stuck payment, and hand you to a person when that is the better answer.'
}[_kind]) %}

{# ── THE COMPACT LINE'S SECOND CLAUSE ────────────────────────────────────────
   It used to print the support mailbox in full, in plain text, directly beneath
   the pay button. Three things wrong with that in that position:

   · It is a worse answer than the link beside it. The assistant re-checks the
     payment with the gateway on the spot; an email is a wait, and offering both
     side by side invites the slower one.
   · A long address in a narrow ballot column had nothing to wrap on, so it ran
     under the edge of the panel on a phone — the client's screenshot shows it
     cut in half.
   · It is a live mailto in public HTML on one of the most-visited pages on the
     platform, which is a gift to address harvesters.

   What belongs there instead is the answer to the question somebody actually has
   with their thumb over a pay button: why does a vote carry a contribution at
   all. That is /philosophy. Overridable, so a caller in a different context can
   point the clause somewhere that fits it.

   The card form (below) still offers "Email the team", where it sits in an
   already-wide layout beside a heading that explains what it is for. #}
{% set _altHref  = sp_alt_href|default('/philosophy') %}
{% set _altLabel = sp_alt_label|default('why a vote carries a contribution') %}
{% if sp_compact|default(false) %}
  <p class="ag-sprompt ag-sprompt--line">
    <a href="{{ _href }}">{{ _title }}</a>
    <span>Or read <a href="{{ _altHref }}">{{ _altLabel }}</a>.</span>
  </p>
{% else %}
  <aside class="ag-sprompt" aria-label="Get help">
    <span class="ag-sprompt__i" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg width="17" height="17" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"
           stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" focusable="false">
        <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><path d="M9.6 9.2a2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4.9.6c0 1.7-2.5 2-2.5 3.7M12 17h.01"/>
      </svg>
    </span>
    <div class="ag-sprompt__b">
      <strong>{{ _title }}</strong>
      <p>{{ _body }}</p>
      <div class="ag-sprompt__a">
        <a class="ag-sprompt__go" href="{{ _href }}">{{ _ref ? 'Re-check it now' : 'Open the assistant' }}</a>
        <a class="ag-sprompt__alt" href="mailto:{{ support_email }}{% if _ref %}?subject={{ ('Payment ' ~ _ref)|url_encode }}{% endif %}">Email the team</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </aside>
{% endif %}

{# Styles ship WITH the component rather than from the global sheet, because it
   is included on pages with wildly different palettes — a dark ballot hero, a
   white success card, an error page that loads no page CSS at all — and one of
   those has no stylesheet to add a rule to. So it carries literal values and
   inherits nothing it cannot afford to lose.

   A page that includes the prompt twice ships this block twice. That is a few
   hundred duplicate bytes and identical rules, which is the cheaper half of the
   trade: the alternative is a global stylesheet the error pages never load, and
   an unstyled support offer on the page where support is most needed. There is
   no once-guard because `{% include %}` gets its own scope — a flag set here
   would not be visible to the next include, so it would guard nothing. #}
<style nonce="{{ csp_nonce }}">
  .ag-sprompt{ display:flex; gap:12px; align-items:flex-start; max-width:640px;
               margin:20px auto 0; padding:14px 16px; text-align:left;
               background:#f6f9f6; border:1px solid rgba(16,41,44,.1); border-radius:14px; }
  .ag-sprompt__i{ flex:none; display:flex; width:30px; height:30px; border-radius:50%;
                  align-items:center; justify-content:center; background:#e6f2e6; color:#1c6b1c; }
  .ag-sprompt__b{ min-width:0; }
  .ag-sprompt strong{ display:block; font-size:14.5px; font-weight:700; color:#10292C; }
  .ag-sprompt p{ margin:4px 0 0; font-size:13.5px; line-height:1.55; color:#4a5c5e; }
  .ag-sprompt__a{ display:flex; gap:14px; flex-wrap:wrap; align-items:center; margin-top:10px; }
  .ag-sprompt__go{ display:inline-block; padding:8px 16px; border-radius:999px; background:#237b22;
                   color:#fff; font-weight:700; font-size:13.5px; text-decoration:none; }
  .ag-sprompt__go:hover{ background:#1c6b1c; }
  .ag-sprompt__alt{ font-weight:600; font-size:13.5px; color:#4a5c5e; text-decoration:underline; }
  .ag-sprompt__go:focus-visible, .ag-sprompt__alt:focus-visible,
  .ag-sprompt--line a:focus-visible{ outline:2px solid #237b22; outline-offset:2px; }
  /* The compact form drops into columns with their own type scale, and a bare
     `font-size` on the wrapper loses to any page rule that targets the anchor
     directly — which is how one sentence came to render as a heavy 16px green
     slab in the middle of a checkout form. Size and weight are therefore set on
     the DESCENDANTS too. This is an aside, not a call to action; the only thing
     that should carry emphasis is the link, and only by being a link. */
  .ag-sprompt--line{ display:block; max-width:none; margin:12px 0 0; padding:0; background:none;
                     border:0; font:400 12.5px/1.6 inherit; color:#5a6d6f; }
  .ag-sprompt--line a, .ag-sprompt--line span{ font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.6; }
  .ag-sprompt--line a{ color:#237b22; font-weight:600; text-decoration:underline;
                       text-underline-offset:2px; }
  .ag-sprompt--line span{ color:inherit; font-weight:400; }

  /* On a dark hero the card would be a white slab. Opt in with `.ag-sprompt--dark`
     on an ancestor rather than guessing from a media query — the page knows what
     colour it is and this component does not. */
  .ag-sprompt--dark .ag-sprompt,
  .ag-sprompt--dark.ag-sprompt{ background:rgba(255,255,255,.06); border-color:rgba(255,255,255,.16); }
  .ag-sprompt--dark .ag-sprompt strong{ color:#fff; }
  .ag-sprompt--dark .ag-sprompt p{ color:rgba(255,255,255,.74); }
  .ag-sprompt--dark .ag-sprompt__i{ background:rgba(127,200,124,.16); color:#7FC87C; }
  .ag-sprompt--dark .ag-sprompt__alt{ color:rgba(255,255,255,.74); }
</style>
